WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:09.280
Cognitive Behavioral Therapy is based on the idea&nbsp;
that certain situations trigger false core beliefs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.240
that negatively impact our thoughts,&nbsp;
emotions, behavior, and physical reactions.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:19.760
Once we learn how to identify what situations&nbsp;
bring upon such destructive thoughts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:24.720
we can practice developing new interpretations&nbsp;
that will then change our pattern of reaction.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.240
The therapy is widely used to help people with&nbsp;
phobias, depression, anxieties, or addictions.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.960
To show how it works, let's look at Lily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:41.360
a teenage girl who hates going to school due&nbsp;
to her fear of being judged and humiliated.

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:49.120
In her first session, the therapist tries to&nbsp;
build trust and explains how CBT functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:54.080
since the better Lily understands the process, the&nbsp;
more likely it is that the therapy is effective.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.120
The therapist also illustrates how our&nbsp;
brain, in specific situations, follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.160
a fixed path of reason — which gets stronger&nbsp;
after years of having the same thought process.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.800
Many of our destructive behaviors are&nbsp;
based on false core beliefs — thoughts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:15.440
that objectively don't make sense. We&nbsp;
acquired these false beliefs when we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.360
were too young to interpret others&nbsp;
correctly. Throughout the therapy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.320
Lily will try to unlearn these false beliefs&nbsp;
and create new mental pathways that will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.080
replace the false beliefs she holds of&nbsp;
herself with more realistic thoughts.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:34.480
Once Lily understands the process,&nbsp;
the counselor begins to ask questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.880
following the Socratic method, a form&nbsp;
of argumentative conversation that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.040
stimulates critical thinking to draw out&nbsp;
false ideas and underlying assumptions.

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:48.240
“Would you like to tell me why you&nbsp;
are here today?” Starts the therapist.&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.800
“Because I think I’m not normal,” Lily responds.
Therapist1: You appear perfectly normal to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.560
can you be more specific?
Lily1: I think I’m afraid of people.&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.000
Therapist2: So, you are afraid of me?
Lily2: No&nbsp;

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.840
Therapist3: Do you feel socially insecure?
Lily3: I’m not sure what you mean.&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.240
Therapist4: Tell me how you feel about school.
Lily4: I’m scared of going because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:13.280
think I’m stupid.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.920
Throughout the interview, the counselor takes&nbsp;
notes of Lily’s answers and identifies the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:25.120
signs of social anxiety based on a false&nbsp;
core belief - Lily believes she is stupid.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.760
For homework, Lily should practice introspection.&nbsp;
The goal: to find out which situations trigger her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:37.840
negative thoughts. She gets a learning&nbsp;
journal to keep record of all triggers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.960
and other observations, such as self-talk or&nbsp;
interpretations of particular events and people.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:50.240
During the following week, Lily becomes more aware&nbsp;
of her thoughts and the physical reactions they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:55.360
trigger. By paying attention to her feelings,&nbsp;
she identifies a specific pattern which occurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.480
every time during math class. The moment her&nbsp;
teacher begins to ask questions her heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:04.960
starts racing and her palms get sweaty - she&nbsp;
worries about having to answer the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.080
about making a mistake, about looking&nbsp;
dumb in front of all the others.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.480
In her second session, Lily shares her&nbsp;
observations and the therapist helps her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:20.480
realize that her cognitive-behavioral patterns&nbsp;
are false. First, her math grades are great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:24.560
so she should feel anything but stupid.&nbsp;
Second, she explains that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:29.920
always more interpretations to the same thing.&nbsp;
What to her may look like her "stupid" face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:35.360
to others she may just look unhappy about having&nbsp;
to answer. The reason she is afraid of what people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.560
think is a form of social anxiety — a completely&nbsp;
irrational cognitive behavioral response.[5.7]

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.920
As the sessions continue, the therapist&nbsp;
suggests three practical strategies:&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:52.560
Through Journaling, Lily records her negative&nbsp;
beliefs and reformulates them into positive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:56.160
ones she can replace them with.
Constructive Self-talk, helps her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:02.000
to replace a critical voice with a positive one.
And she starts exposure exercises, which means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
Lilly deliberately puts herself in situations&nbsp;
where she becomes the center of attention.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.760
Along the way, the two set goals that&nbsp;
are specific, measurable, achievable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.840
realistic, and time-based. SMART goals&nbsp;
give her control over how she progresses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:22.000
thus helping her to gain confidence in herself.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:30.000
Over time and with a lot of practise, her brain&nbsp;
builds new neural pathways that lead to different,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:33.840
more neutral reactions to the&nbsp;
same old triggers. And one day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.240
Lily may even enjoy the thrill of speaking&nbsp;
in front of her class. Her interpretation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:42.640
of the situation is more realistic and&nbsp;
more aligned with those of the others.

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:52.160
CBT was initially developed in 1964 by Aaron&nbsp;
Temkin Beck. Beck hypothesised that people's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.360
feelings are determined by the way they interpret&nbsp;
situations, rather than by the situations per se.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:03.680
About depression he once said: “If our thinking&nbsp;
is bogged down by distorted symbolic meanings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:09.840
illogical reasoning and erroneous interpretations,&nbsp;
we become, in truth, blind and deaf”.

